{"content":[{"c":["CISTERN"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Place to store water; a man-made catch basin or reservoir. Stone cisterns plastered with lime came into common use in Palestine in the 13th century ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Leaky or abandoned cisterns were often used as burial, torture, or prison chambers. For example, the dungeon into which the prophet Jeremiah was lowered was an abandoned muddy cistern (",{"href":"?bref=Jer.38.6","r":"Jer.38.6","rawR":"Jer.38.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.38.6","value":"Jer.38.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 38:6"},"). Ishmael threw the bodies of 70 murdered men into a large cistern originally constructed by King Asa for a wartime water supply (",{"href":"?bref=Jer.41.4-9","r":"Jer.41.4-9","rawR":"Jer.41.4-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.41.4-9","value":"Jer.41.4-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"41:4-9"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Cisterns were vitally important in the arid Near East. King Uzziah of Judah is described as hewing out many cisterns in areas where springs or wells were lacking (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.26.10","r":"2Chr.26.10","rawR":"2Chr.26.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.26.10","value":"2Chr.26.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 26:10"},"). An Assyrian general taunting King Hezekiah and his people promised that, if they would submit, everyone would drink the water of his own cistern (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.36.16","r":"Isa.36.16","rawR":"Isa.36.16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.36.16","value":"Isa.36.16"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 36:16"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.18.31","r":"2Kgs.18.31","rawR":"2Kgs.18.31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.18.31","value":"2Kgs.18.31"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 18:31"},"). Much earlier, Moses had assured the Israelites that cisterns already hewn out would be among God’s blessing in the Promised Land (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.6.11","r":"Deut.6.11","rawR":"Deut.6.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.6.11","value":"Deut.6.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 6:11"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Cistern","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Jer 38:6","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Jer.38.6","rawQueryValue":"Jer.38.6","sourceRefExact":"Jer.38.6"},{"anchorText":"41:4-9","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Jer.41.4-9","rawQueryValue":"Jer.41.4-9","sourceRefExact":"Jer.41.4-9"},{"anchorText":"2 Chr 26:10","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Chr.26.10","rawQueryValue":"2Chr.26.10","sourceRefExact":"2Chr.26.10"},{"anchorText":"Is 36:16","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Isa.36.16","rawQueryValue":"Isa.36.16","sourceRefExact":"Isa.36.16"},{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 18:31","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.18.31","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.18.31","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.18.31"},{"anchorText":"Dt 6:11","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Deut.6.11","rawQueryValue":"Deut.6.11","sourceRefExact":"Deut.6.11"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"47e58636282730c4956e9ba9656c32466619be7492e8e62a97aace7e267066ed","sourceItemName":"Cistern","sourceItemOrder":195,"sourceMember":"Articles/C.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[{"name":"FigurativeUseOfCistern","src":"../Textboxes/Textboxes.xml"}]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Cistern","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Cistern","title":"Cistern","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}